# ECS — Entities & Components

Funke organizes everything in your game with an **Entity Component System (ECS)**. This page explains the model: what entities and components are, the components Funke ships with, and how you work with them.

If you just want the short version: an **entity** is an empty "thing" in your scene. You give it **components** (data like a position, a sprite, a collider) to make it *something*. Code then reads and changes those components.

> Funke uses the [EnTT](https://github.com/skypjack/entt) library under the hood, but you rarely touch it directly — Funke wraps it in a friendly `Entity` type.

---

## The three pieces

ECS splits a game object into three separate ideas:

| Piece | What it is | Example |
|-------|-----------|---------|
| **Entity** | An ID — a lightweight handle for "a thing." Has no data of its own. | "Object #42" |
| **Component** | A bundle of data attached to an entity. | `Transform2D { position, rotation, scale }` |
| **System / Script** | Code that runs over entities with certain components. | "Move everything with a `Rigidbody2D`." |

The power of this split: an entity is whatever its components say it is. Add a `SpriteRenderer` and it draws; add a `Rigidbody2D` and it falls; remove them and it stops. You compose behavior instead of inheriting it.

---

## Entities

An **entity** is essentially an ID. In code, Funke gives you an `Entity` handle that bundles that ID with the scene it belongs to, so you can call methods on it.

Creating and using an entity in C++:

```cpp
Entity e = Entity::Create("Player");      // make a named entity
e.AddComponent<SpriteRendererComponent>(); // give it a sprite
auto& tr = e.GetComponent<Transform2DComponent>();
tr.SetPosition({ 5.0f, 0.0f });
```

The same idea in a C# script:

```csharp
Entity e = Entity.Create("Player");
e.AddComponent<SpriteRenderer>();
e.Transform.Position = new Vector2(5f, 0f);
```

The core entity operations are the same everywhere:

- `AddComponent<T>()` / `RemoveComponent<T>()`
- `GetComponent<T>()` / `HasComponent<T>()`
- `Create(...)` / `Destroy()`
- parent/child: `SetParent(...)`, `GetParent()`, get children

### Entity identity

Entities carry a bit of bookkeeping so the engine knows where each one came from:

- a **name** (the `NameComponent`),
- a stable **UUID** for entities saved in a scene,
- an **origin**: was this entity authored in a **Scene**, spawned from a **Prefab**, or created at **Runtime**?

This is how the engine knows, for example, that runtime-spawned entities shouldn't be written back into your scene file.

---

## Components

A component is just **data**. In C++ they're plain structs — no base class required:

```cpp
struct HealthComponent {
    float Current = 100.0f;
    float Max     = 100.0f;
};
```

Some components hold no data at all — these are **tags**, used purely as markers (for example a `DisabledTag` that flags an entity as turned off). Code can quickly ask "give me every entity with this tag."

### Built-in components

Funke ships a large set of components, grouped by area. Here are the main ones:

**General**
- `Transform2D` — position, rotation, and scale (the most fundamental component). Supports local *and* world space, and follows its parent in a hierarchy.
- `Name` — the entity's display name.
- `Hierarchy` — parent and children links.
- `RectTransform2D` — a UI-oriented transform for layout.

**Graphics**
- `SpriteRenderer` — draws a texture/sprite.
- `Camera2D` — a 2D camera (what the player sees).
- `TextRenderer` — draws text with a font.
- `Image` — a UI image. **Image Type = Sliced** (9-slice) keeps **Border Left/Right/Top/Bottom** (texture pixels, scaled by **Border Scale**) at their size while the middle stretches, so one rounded-rectangle sprite fits buttons and panels of any size. Border Scale gives UI units, so the borders follow the UI scale (reference resolution) and the rect's own scale just like its size: a sliced panel looks the same in a small window and on a large screen. **Image Type = Tiled** keeps the corners as well but repeats the edges and the middle at **Border Scale** UI units per texture pixel instead of stretching them (without a border the whole sprite repeats). When all four borders are 0, the image uses the border stored with its texture: the texture Inspector's **Border** for a whole image, or the Sprite Editor's per-slice border for a sprite slice (`.meta`: `"border": [left, bottom, right, top]`; `funke sprite border` sets it from the command line). **Material** takes a `.material` whose shader targets `UI` (see *Custom Shaders*). Scene keys: `imageType` (1 = Sliced, 2 = Tiled), `border` `[left, bottom, right, top]`, `borderScale`, `materialAsset`. C#: `Image.Type`, `Image.Border` (X left, Y bottom, Z right, W top), `Image.BorderScale`, `Image.Material`.
- `ParticleSystem2D` — particle effects.
- `ParticleSystem3D` — 3D billboard particle effects (see [3D](3D.md#particles)).
- `PostProcessing2D` — screen effects like bloom or vignette.

**Physics**
- `Rigidbody2D` — makes an entity participate in physics.
- `BoxCollider2D`, `CircleCollider2D`, `PolygonCollider2D` — collision shapes.
- `RevoluteJoint2D`, `PrismaticJoint2D`, `WeldJoint2D` — connect two bodies (see [Physics 2D](Physics-2D.md)).
- `Rigidbody3D`, `BoxCollider3D`, `SphereCollider3D`, `CapsuleCollider3D`, `MeshCollider3D` — the 3D equivalents (Jolt Physics, see [Physics 3D](Physics-3D.md)).

**UI**
- `Button`, `Slider`, `Toggle`, `InputField`, `Dropdown`, `ScrollRect`, layout groups, and more.

UI labels use `Funke.TextRenderer` (`using Funke;`). It lives in the root `Funke` namespace because the same component renders world and UI text; widgets such as `Button` and `Slider` live in `Funke.UI`.

**Audio**
- `AudioSource` — plays a sound from an entity, flat (2D) or as 3D sound from the entity's position.

You add any of these in the editor through the **Add Component** menu, or in code with `AddComponent<T>()`.

### How components become "known"

For a component to appear in the editor and save/load with scenes, it must be **registered** — given a display name, a category, a serialized name, and a list of editable fields. The engine registers all its built-ins at startup; **packages register their own** the same way (see [Writing Packages](../Packages/Writing-Packages.md)).

Registration is also where the engine declares rules like "these two components can't be on the same entity at once" (conflicts), which the Add Component menu respects.

---

## Hierarchy (parents and children)

Entities can be nested. A child's `Transform2D` is **relative to its parent**, so moving a parent moves all its children with it. This is stored with the `Hierarchy` component (a parent link plus a list of children).

```csharp
child.SetParent(parent);   // child now follows parent
```

In the editor you build hierarchy by dragging entities onto each other in the Scene Hierarchy panel.

> **Scripting note:** when you move an entity by writing directly to a native transform reference, you may need to flag the transform as "dirty" so children update. The higher-level `Transform` API does this for you.

---

## How entities are stored

All the entities of a scene live in that scene's **registry** (the EnTT world). Each [Scene](Scenes.md) owns its own registry, so entities in one scene are completely separate from another's.

Components of the same type are stored together in tight arrays, which makes iterating over "every entity with component X" very fast.

---

## Working with many entities

Game logic usually runs over *all* entities that have a certain set of components. This is what **systems** (C++) and **scripts** (C#) do.

In C#, you query a scene for the combination you care about:

```csharp
// Every entity that has both a Transform2D and a SpriteRenderer:
foreach (ref var t in Scene.QueryRef<NativeTransform2D>())
{
    t.LocalRotation += Time.DeltaTime;  // spin everything
}
```

For a single entity, `entity.TransformRef` (or `GetNativeComponent<T>()`) returns a writable `ref` straight into component memory. When you only **read** a transform, use `entity.TransformRefReadOnly` (or `GetNativeComponentReadOnly<T>()`): a writable transform ref makes the engine re-check every transform in the scene that frame, a read-only one does not.

```csharp
ref readonly var self = ref Entity.TransformRefReadOnly;
ref readonly var target = ref Target.TransformRefReadOnly;   // Target: an Entity field
float distance = Vector2.Distance(self.Position, target.Position);
```

In C++, a system iterates a *view* of the registry:

```cpp
auto view = registry.view<Transform2DComponent, Rigidbody2DComponent>();
for (auto entity : view) {
    auto& tr = view.get<Transform2DComponent>(entity);
    // ...move it...
}
```

For details on **systems** (the C++ side, with `Awake`/`Start`/`Update`) see [Scenes](Scenes.md#systems). For **scripts** (the C# side) see [Scripting](../Scripting/Scripting.md). For running queries across many threads, see the [Job System](Job-System.md).

### Batched structural changes in C#

`EntityCommandBuffer` records entity creation, destruction, and component additions/removals. Call `Playback()` on the **main thread** in the executing scene; it returns after the entire batch has finished. Supported native data operations can use workers during that call. Recording does not change the scene.

Component operations use **unmanaged `IComponent` structs**: built-in types such as `Funke.Native.NativeTransform2D` and `NativeSpriteRenderer`, or registered custom structs. Managed wrapper classes such as `Transform2D` and `SpriteRenderer` do not satisfy this constraint.

```csharp
using Funke;
using Funke.Native;

public static class EcbExample
{
    public static Entity Apply(Entity addTo, Entity removeFrom, Entity destroy)
    {
        using var ecb = new EntityCommandBuffer();
        EntityRef spawned = ecb.CreateWith<NativeTransform2D, NativeSpriteRenderer>();

        ecb.AddComponent<NativeSpriteRenderer>(addTo);
        EntityRef live = addTo.ToCommandBufferRef();
        var sprite = new NativeSpriteRenderer { Color = new Color(1f, 0.5f, 0f, 1f) };
        ecb.AddComponent(live, in sprite);
        ecb.RemoveComponent<NativeSpriteRenderer>(removeFrom);
        ecb.Destroy(destroy.ToCommandBufferRef());

        ecb.Playback();
        return ecb.GetCreatedEntity((int)spawned.Index);
    }
}
```

Both `AddComponent` overloads and `RemoveComponent` accept a live `Entity` or an `EntityRef`. Local refs from `Create()` / `CreateWith<...>()` work too; `Destroy` accepts an `EntityRef`. Live references are resolved at playback, and references to entities already destroyed or outside the executing scene are skipped. Removing an absent component does nothing.

The default and payload forms have different initialization rules:

| Operation | Initialization at playback |
|-----------|----------------------------|
| `CreateWith<T>()` or `AddComponent<T>(localRef)` | Uses native member defaults and registered dependencies/conflicts, without the root component's smart-default callback. |
| `AddComponent<T>(liveEntityOrRef)` | Uses the normal registered add path, including dependencies, conflicts, and smart defaults. An existing component keeps its value. |
| `AddComponent(entityOrRef, in data)` | Copies `data` when recorded, then adds or replaces the value at playback. Root smart defaults do not overwrite that payload. Constructors and initializers run before recording; `default(T)` zeroes fields. |

`Playback()` returns the number of recorded entity creations, so a batch that only modifies existing entities returns `0`. Reuse a buffer with `Clear()` before recording the next batch; playback does not clear it. Native component references must be reacquired after structural changes. A managed wrapper for a removed component becomes invalid; fetch a new wrapper after re-adding it.

Choose playback execution with `ecb.Playback(EntityCommandBufferPlaybackMode.Parallel)` or the equivalent mode argument on `PlaybackInto(destination, mode)`:

| Mode | Behavior |
|------|----------|
| `Automatic` | Default. Selects native parallel playback for sufficiently large supported batches. |
| `Sequential` | Uses the ordered serial playback path. Useful when comparing performance. |
| `Parallel` | Requests the supported native parallel path. Unsupported batches still fall back; small kernels or unavailable workers may execute inline. |

The staged native path supports creation and eligible additions/removals of `NativeTransform2D` and `NativeSpriteRenderer`, plus destruction of entities containing only supported components. UUID candidate generation, component initialization, and disjoint payload moves/destruction can run on workers. Entity-handle allocation, storage bookkeeping, identity-index commits, and lifecycle callbacks remain serial on the main thread.

Batches requiring prefab loading, hierarchy or script lifecycle handling, custom component logic, extra callbacks, or ordering between mixed operations use the ordered path. Playback is synchronous in every mode. Finish all jobs that access the scene before playback; native parallel playback does not make direct entity/component structural calls thread-safe.

### Recording from parallel jobs

`AsParallelWriter()` exposes the same creation, destruction, and component operations to workers. Prepare live refs before scheduling, finish all writers, then call `Playback()` on the main thread:

```csharp
using Funke;
using Funke.Jobs;
using Funke.Native;

public static class ParallelEcbExample
{
    private struct RemoveSpritesJob : IJobParallelFor
    {
        public EntityCommandBuffer.ParallelWriter Writer;
        public EntityRef[] Entities;

        public void Execute(int index)
            => Writer.RemoveComponent<NativeSpriteRenderer>(Entities[index]);
    }

    public static void RemoveSprites(Entity[] entities)
    {
        var refs = new EntityRef[entities.Length];
        for (int i = 0; i < entities.Length; ++i)
            refs[i] = entities[i].ToCommandBufferRef();

        using var ecb = new EntityCommandBuffer();
        var job = new RemoveSpritesJob { Writer = ecb.AsParallelWriter(), Entities = refs };
        Job.ScheduleParallelFor(job, refs.Length, batchSize: 256).Complete();
        ecb.Playback();
    }
}
```

Live refs can be shared between workers. A local ref returned by a worker's `Create()` / `CreateWith<...>()` belongs to that worker and must stay there. Commands retain order within each recorder; do not rely on ordering between workers. Keep dependent operations such as remove-then-add for one entity in the same recorder. Call `Clear()` or `Dispose()` only after all writers have finished.

### Batched structural changes in C++

`NativeEntityCommandBuffer` supports `CreateEntity()`, `CreateWith<T...>()`, `Instantiate()`, `AddComponent<T>()`, `RemoveComponent<T>()`, `Destroy()` and `SetEnabled()`. Use `NativeEntityCommandBuffer::Existing(entity)` to capture a live target before scheduling workers. A live reference retains the scene lifetime and entity generation without keeping the scene alive. Local creation references belong to their recorder and become invalid after `Clear()`.

`ParallelEntityCommandBuffer` merges independent recorders and uses the same native playback backend as C#. This example removes sprite components from existing entities:

```cpp
#include "Components/Graphics/SpriteRendererComponent.hpp"
#include "Jobs/ParallelFor.hpp"
#include "Scripting/ParallelEntityCommandBuffer.hpp"

void RemoveSprites(Funke::Scene& scene, std::span<const Funke::Entity> entities) {
    using namespace Funke;
    std::vector<NativeEntityCommandBuffer::EntityRef> refs;
    refs.reserve(entities.size());
    for (const Entity& entity : entities)
        refs.push_back(NativeEntityCommandBuffer::Existing(entity));

    ParallelEntityCommandBuffer commands;
    ParallelFor(0, refs.size(), [&](size_t begin, size_t end) {
        auto& writer = commands.AsWriter();
        for (size_t i = begin; i < end; ++i)
            writer.RemoveComponent<SpriteRendererComponent>(refs[i]);
    }, 256);
    commands.Playback(scene, EntityCommandBufferPlaybackMode::Parallel);
}
```

`AsWriter()` selects a slot for the current JobSystem worker or the thread that constructed the buffer. For external threads, construct `ParallelEntityCommandBuffer commands(writerCount)` and give each producer a distinct `commands.AsWriter(index)`. Join all producers before playback, inspection or clearing. Do not mix explicit slot ownership with automatic JobSystem slot selection during one recording interval.

Playback runs synchronously on the main thread. `Auto` chooses the supported parallel path for sufficiently large batches; `Sequential` requests ordered serial execution; `Parallel` requests native parallel playback with a serial fallback. Supported default native component operations can run in parallel. Custom components, value/configure callbacks, prefab loading and dependent mixed operations retain ordered execution. The typed native value/configure overloads retain their existing C++ construction behavior.

Commands preserve order inside a recorder; recorders merge in slot order. Job scheduling may choose different slots between runs, so put operations that depend on each other in the same recorder. Direct `Entity::AddComponent`/`RemoveComponent` calls remain structural operations on the main thread.

The playback scene must match every recorded live target. A mismatched or expired scene is rejected before mutation; destroyed/recycled target handles are skipped. Native playback retains generation-checked handles throughout execution, so clone/paste UUID remapping cannot redirect a recorded native target. Playback returns the number of recorded creations (`0` for mutation-only batches), or a negative backend error code. `GetCreatedEntity(index)` returns a native entity handle; `Clear()` retains buffer capacity for reuse. Recompile native script DLLs after this API update (native script ABI 5).

Native commands use stable pages of 256 commands (16 KiB per allocated page); captures keep their addresses as the buffer grows. Buffers without nontrivial captures clear without scanning their commands. Playback still validates components and serializes supported commands for the shared backend, but skips UUID conversions for native targets and results. Parallel recording does not guarantee a lower total cost than direct typed C++ calls.

---

## Summary

- An **entity** is an ID; **components** are the data attached to it; **systems/scripts** are the code.
- Components are plain data; empty ones are **tags**.
- Funke ships components for transforms, graphics, physics, UI, and audio; packages add more.
- Each **scene** owns its entities. Entities can form **parent/child** hierarchies.
- You work with entities via `AddComponent` / `GetComponent` and query many at once with views (C++) or `Query` (C#).

## Related pages

- [Scenes](Scenes.md) — the container entities live in, plus systems and their lifecycle.
- [Scripting](../Scripting/Scripting.md) — writing C# logic over entities and components.
- [Writing Packages](../Packages/Writing-Packages.md) — adding your own components.
- [Job System](Job-System.md) — processing many entities in parallel.

Github-Docs/Documentation/Engine-Core/Ph:
# Physics 3D

3D entities are simulated by [Jolt Physics](https://github.com/jrouwe/JoltPhysics), the engine behind *Horizon Forbidden West* and the default 3D physics of Godot. It runs multi-threaded and is statically linked into `Funke-Engine.dll`. 2D physics (Box2D, plus the optional Funke.Physics package) is separate and unchanged; the two stacks never interact, because an entity has either a Transform 2D or a Transform 3D.

## Components

All of them need a **Transform 3D** and are listed under **Physics 3D** in the Add Component menu.

| Component | What it does |
|---|---|
| **Rigidbody 3D** | Makes the entity a physics body. **Body Type**: *Dynamic* (gravity and forces move it), *Kinematic* (moves only where its Transform 3D is set, pushes dynamic bodies) or *Static*. Also Mass, Linear/Angular Drag, Gravity Scale, Continuous Collision, **Interpolate** (on by default, see below) and per-axis Freeze Position/Rotation. |
| **Box Collider 3D** | Box with **Size** (local; the entity's scale applies on top). |
| **Sphere Collider 3D** | Sphere with **Radius**. A non-uniform scale uses the largest axis. |
| **Capsule Collider 3D** | Capsule with **Radius**, **Height** (end to end) and **Direction** (X/Y/Z). |
| **Mesh Collider 3D** | Collides with a mesh: the entity's Mesh Renderer mesh, including its custom `.obj` mesh (**Use Mesh Renderer**), or its own **Custom Mesh** or primitive. **Convex** uses the convex hull. |

Every collider also has **Center** (local offset), **Is Trigger**, **Friction** and **Bounciness**.

In the 3D Editor View the selected entity's colliders are outlined (green, triggers light blue) with the size and scale physics uses, also through meshes. Mesh colliders show their triangle edges; a convex custom mesh shows its bounds, since its hull is built when play starts.

### How bodies are formed

- An entity with a **Rigidbody 3D** is one body. Its own colliders **and the colliders on child entities without their own Rigidbody 3D** are combined into that body's shape, so complex objects can be built from several primitives on child entities.
- A collider with no Rigidbody 3D on itself or any ancestor is a **static** body. Moving its Transform 3D (script or inspector) teleports it.
- A non-convex Mesh Collider only works on static and kinematic bodies. On a dynamic Rigidbody 3D it falls back to its convex hull and logs a warning.
- A body is a trigger only when **all** of its colliders have Is Trigger set.
- Friction is averaged over a body's colliders; Bounciness uses the highest value.

### Edit mode vs. play mode

Bodies only exist in play mode (and always in the Runtime). The scene's physics world is created on the first fixed step that finds a 3D physics component and is discarded when play stops, so nothing from a play session leaks into the next one. Editing a collider or Rigidbody 3D field during play rebuilds the body and keeps its velocity.

Dynamic bodies write their position and rotation back into the Transform 3D after every fixed step. Setting a Transform 3D's position from a script teleports a dynamic body; for a kinematic body it becomes a smooth move over the next step, so it still pushes dynamic bodies correctly.

**Interpolate:** physics steps at the fixed tick rate (50 Hz by default), usually slower than the screen refreshes. With Interpolate on, a dynamic body's Transform 3D is blended between its last two physics steps every frame, before scripts run, so the body and a camera that follows it in `OnUpdate` move smoothly. The cost is one physics step of visual delay. Scripts read the blended pose; setting the position still teleports the body.

Physics steps at the project's fixed tick rate (see `FunkeProject::PhysicsTickRate`). Steps longer than 1/60 s are split into several collision sub-steps.

## Scripting

```csharp
using Funke;
using Funke.Physics;

public class Ball : EntityScript
{
    public override void OnStart()
    {
        var body = GetComponent<Rigidbody3D>()!;
        body.AddForce(new Vector3(0, 5, 0), ForceMode3D.Impulse);
        body.Constraints = RigidbodyConstraints3D.FreezeRotationX | RigidbodyConstraints3D.FreezeRotationZ;
    }

    public override void OnFixedUpdate()
    {
        if (Physics3D.Raycast(GetComponent<Transform3D>()!.Position, new Vector3(0, -1, 0), out RaycastHit3D hit, 2.0f))
            Log.Info($"Ground {hit.Entity} at {hit.Distance}");
    }

    public override void OnCollisionEnter3D(Collision3D collision)
    {
        if (collision.Normal.Y > 0.7f) Log.Info($"Landed on {collision.Other}");
    }

    public override void OnTriggerEnter3D(Collision3D other) => Log.Info($"Entered {other.Other}");
}
```

- **Rigidbody3D**: `BodyType`, `Mass`, `LinearDrag`, `AngularDrag`, `GravityScale`, `ContinuousCollision`, `Constraints`, `LinearVelocity`, `AngularVelocity` (radians/s), `AddForce`, `AddForceAtPosition`, `AddTorque` (with `ForceMode3D.Force / Impulse / Acceleration / VelocityChange`), `IsSleeping`, `WakeUp()`, `Sleep()`.
- **BoxCollider3D / SphereCollider3D / CapsuleCollider3D / MeshCollider3D**: every inspector field.
- **Physics3D**: `Raycast(origin, direction, out RaycastHit3D hit, maxDistance)` or `Raycast(Ray ray, out hit, maxDistance)` hits the closest non-trigger collider. `RaycastHit3D.Entity` is the entity owning the collider, which can be a child of the Rigidbody 3D. `RaycastFromScreen(screenPoint, out hit)` picks what is under a screen point through `Camera3D.Main`. `Physics3D.Gravity` defaults to (0, -9.81, 0) and resets when play restarts.

### Picking with the mouse or a finger

`Camera3D.ScreenPointToRay(screenPoint)` turns a screen position into a `Ray` (`Origin` on the near plane, normalized `Direction`, `GetPoint(distance)`). Screen positions are viewport pixels with the origin at the top left, the same as `Input.MousePosition` and `Touch.Position`, also inside the editor's Game View. Orthographic cameras give parallel rays. `Camera3D.WorldToScreenPoint(worldPoint, out Vector3 screen)` goes the other way (for labels or markers over 3D objects): `screen.Z` is the distance in front of the camera, and the call returns false for points behind it.

```csharp
if (Input.GetMouseButtonDown(MouseButton.Left) && Physics3D.RaycastFromScreen(Input.MousePosition, out RaycastHit3D hit))
    Log.Info($"Clicked {hit.Entity.Name} at {hit.Point}");
```
- **Callbacks** (on scripts of the body's entity, i.e. the Rigidbody 3D entity or the static collider's entity): `OnCollisionEnter3D/Stay3D/Exit3D(Collision3D)` and `OnTriggerEnter3D/Stay3D/Exit3D(Collision3D)`. Contacts are reported once per body pair. `Collision3D.Normal` points from `Other` toward `Self`. A body falling asleep doesn't end a contact, and while it sleeps it gets no Stay events. Destroying or disabling a body sends Exit to the other side.

Velocity, forces and raycasts do nothing in edit mode. Scripts run their `OnFixedUpdate` before the physics step, so forces added there apply in the same step.

## Engine side

| Path | Contents |
|---|---|
| `Funke-Engine/src/Components/Physics3D/` | `Rigidbody3DComponent`, `Collider3DComponents` (plain data, no Jolt types) |
| `Funke-Engine/src/Physics3D/PhysicsWorld3D.*` | One Jolt `PhysicsSystem` per scene: body sync, stepping, contact pairing, raycasts (Jolt is hidden behind a pimpl) |
| `Funke-Engine/src/Physics3D/PhysicsSystem3D.*` | The scene system that owns the world in play mode (one of the standard scene systems) |
| `Funke-Engine/src/Scene/BuiltInComponentRegistrationPhysics3D.cpp` | Inspector properties and JSON serialization |
| `Funke-Engine/src/Scripting/ScriptBindingsPhysics3D.cpp` | C# bindings |
| `premake/dependencies/jolt.lua`, `External/JoltPhysics` | The Jolt build (submodule, pinned to v5.6.0) |

Jolt is compiled without any `JPH_*` feature defines (no debug renderer, profiler, ObjectStream or GPU compute), so engine code that includes Jolt headers needs none either. If you ever add one, add it to the Jolt project **and** every consumer, because a mismatch changes object layouts.

## Limitations

- Only the built-in primitives (Cube, Sphere, Plane, Cylinder) can be used as mesh colliders, because those are the meshes the Mesh Renderer draws.
- No joints/constraints, character controller, collision layers or debug drawing of colliders in the viewport yet.

Github-Docs/Documentation/Getting-Starte:
# Your First Game

This walkthrough takes a new project from the Launcher to a finished desktop build in about 15 minutes. It assumes you have never used Funke before. Screens and names are the same on Windows, Linux and macOS; executables just have no `.exe` outside Windows.

Funke supports **C++ and C#** game development. This walkthrough uses C#; for C++ in the editor, follow [Native C++ scripts](Scripting/Scripting.md#native-c-scripts). For a native application using the SDK directly, see the [C++ application guide](../../Funke-Engine/README.md). A project can combine both languages.

Prefer to explore an existing game? Open the Launcher's **Asset Library** for reference projects such as **NeonStack** and **Voxtau**, plus the **FunkeBench** benchmark sample. These are distributed through the separate [Funke Asset Library](https://github.com/Ben-Scr/Funke-AssetLibrary). The **Empty 2D** and **Empty 3D** templates are blank starting points, not the complete example catalog.

This tutorial uses built-in APIs to keep setup small. For tilemaps, install **Funke.Tilemap2D**; for input actions and saved rebinding, install **Funke.Input** through the editor's **Package Manager**. See the [package overview](README.md#packages) for links and installation guidance.

## 1. Install the prerequisites

| You need | For | Download |
|---|---|---|
| Windows 10/11 x64 with a D3D12- or Vulkan-capable GPU, a 64-bit Linux with a Vulkan driver, or macOS 11+ | running the editor | — |
| **.NET 10 SDK** for your CPU | compiling C# scripts | [dotnet.microsoft.com](https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download/dotnet/10.0) |
| A C++ toolchain: MSVC Build Tools (v143) on Windows, GCC/Clang + make on Linux, Xcode Command Line Tools on macOS | only native C++ scripts and packages | [visualstudio.microsoft.com](https://visualstudio.microsoft.com/visual-cpp-build-tools/) |

The Launcher checks for these on start and shows a **Download** button next to anything that is missing.

## 2. Create a project

1. Start **Funke-Launcher.exe** from the release folder.
2. Click **New Project**, choose **Empty 2D**, enter a name, pick a folder, and confirm.
3. The Launcher creates the project, compiles its scripts once, and lists it under **Projects**. Double-click it to open the editor.

New projects start empty: `Assets/Scenes/SampleScene.scene` holds only a **Camera**, and `Assets/Scripts/GameScript.cs` is an empty script. The next steps build a small game in it: a player you move into a coin.

## 3. Build the scene

1. Right-click the **Hierarchy** and choose **Create › 2D Entity › Physics › Dynamic Body**. Rename it to **Player**. In the Inspector set the **Rigidbody 2D**'s **Gravity Scale** to `0` and enable **Freeze Rotation**, so it does not fall or spin.
2. Choose **Create › 2D Entity › Sprite › Circle** and rename it to **Coin**. Move it to the right of the player, then **Add Component › Circle Collider 2D** and enable **Sensor**: the player passes through it, and the coin is told about the touch.

## 4. Write two scripts

In the **Asset Browser**, open `Assets/Scripts`, right-click and choose **Create › EntityScript**. Name it `PlayerController` and double-click it to open your code editor:

```csharp
using Funke;

public class PlayerController : EntityScript
{
    public float Speed = 5.0f;

    private Rigidbody2D? m_Body;

    public override void OnStart() => m_Body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();

    public override void OnFixedUpdate()
    {
        if (m_Body != null)
            m_Body.LinearVelocity = Input.GetAxis() * Speed;
    }
}
```

- `Speed` is a public field, so it appears in the **Inspector**. Change it there and press Play again.
- Movement is set in `OnFixedUpdate` because it drives physics.

Create a second EntityScript named `Coin`:

```csharp
using Funke;

public class Coin : EntityScript
{
    public override void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collision2D other)
    {
        if (other.Other.Name != "Player")
            return;

        // PlayerPrefs survives restarts.
        int collected = PlayerPrefs.GetInt("CoinsCollected") + 1;
        PlayerPrefs.SetInt("CoinsCollected", collected);
        Log.Info($"Coin collected! Total across all sessions: {collected}");

        Entity.Destroy();
    }
}
```

`OnTriggerEnter2D` is the callback for **sensor** colliders. Save a script and the editor recompiles and reloads it automatically.

Attach the scripts: select the Player, **Add Component**, pick `PlayerController`; do the same for the Coin with `Coin`.

### Press Play

Click **Play** in the toolbar. Move with **WASD**, the arrow keys or a gamepad's left stick, and touch the coin. The Console prints how many coins you have collected across all sessions. Press **Stop** to return to editing; nothing that happened during play is kept.

## 5. Make it yours

Some small next steps, each using one new feature:

1. **Add more coins.** Select the Coin, press **Ctrl+D** to duplicate it, and drag the copies around the Viewport. **Ctrl+Z** undoes any step.
2. **Turn the coin into a prefab.** Drag the Coin from the Hierarchy into the Asset Browser. Changes to the prefab then apply to every instance.
3. **Add art.** Drop a `.png` into `Assets/`, then drag it onto the Player's **Sprite Renderer › Texture** slot. For a sprite sheet, slice it in the **Sprite Editor** and add an **Animator** with a looping clip that records the slices on the **Sprite Renderer > Texture** track; see [Animation](Engine-Core/Animation.md).
4. **Add walls.** Create an entity with a `BoxCollider2D` (no Rigidbody needed for static walls). The player bumps into it because the wall's collider is not a sensor.
5. **Keep score on screen.** Right-click the Hierarchy, choose **Create › UI › Text**, and update its text from the Coin script.

## 6. Build the game

1. Open **Project Settings** and set the executable name, company and version. The company and name also decide where saves live: `%LOCALAPPDATA%\<Company>\<Name>`.
2. Make sure **SampleScene** is in **Build Scenes** and set as the startup scene.
3. Open the **Build** panel, choose the **Release** profile, pick an empty output folder outside the project, and click **Build**.

The output folder is your game. Desktop build profiles include .NET by default, so players do not need to install it separately. Windows players still need the [Visual C++ Redistributable](https://aka.ms/vs/17/release/vc_redist.x64.exe). Turn off **Build Profiles > Include .NET Runtime** only when your installer supplies .NET or your players already have it. See [Building and Shipping](Editor/Building-and-Shipping.md) for the full release checklist.

## Where to go next

- [Editor](Editor/Editor.md): panels, shortcuts, undo, crash recovery.
- [Scripting](Scripting/Scripting.md): lifecycle, input, physics, saving data.
- [Scenes](Engine-Core/Scenes.md) and [ECS](Engine-Core/ECS.md): how a game is organised.
- [Known Limitations](Known-Limitations.md): what the beta does not do yet.

Github-Docs/Documentation/Scripting/Scri:
# Scripting

Funke supports game scripting in **C# and native C++**, and a project can use both. Write scripts to move the player, spawn enemies, handle UI and manage game state in the language that fits your project. The native engine's C++20 implementation does not restrict game development to C++.

The first sections cover C# with `EntityScript`, `SceneSystem` and `GlobalSystem` (.NET 10 on desktop). For C++ gameplay in the editor, jump to [Native C++ scripts](#native-c-scripts), which covers `NativeScript`, Inspector fields and native compilation/reload. C++ is a supported gameplay workflow, not limited to performance optimizations. For a C++ application using the SDK directly, see the [native application guide](../../../Funke-Engine/README.md). Platform runtime differences are documented in [Known Limitations](../Known-Limitations.md).

Visual Studio IntelliSense for Funke game projects requires Visual Studio 2026 (18.0+) because Visual Studio 2022 cannot evaluate `net10.0` projects. VS Code or Rider can use the installed .NET 10 SDK instead.

**Script editor:** Editor Preferences › Scripting › External Script Editor lists every detected Visual Studio, VS Code, JetBrains Rider and IntelliJ IDEA installation (standalone installer, JetBrains Toolbox 1.x and 2.x), each version on its own. Double-clicking a script or a console error opens the file at that line; Rider opens the project's `.sln`, IntelliJ IDEA the project folder. IntelliJ IDEA has no C# language support, so you get editing without code completion; use Rider for that.

If you just want the short version: make a class that inherits from `EntityScript`, override `OnUpdate`, attach it to an entity in the editor, and press Play.

\---

## The three kinds of script

Funke's C# API has three script base classes, each with a different **scope**:

|Base class|Scope|Use it for|
|-|-|-|
|**EntityScript**|One entity|Behavior for a specific object — a player, an enemy, a pickup.|
|**SceneSystem**|One scene|Logic over a whole scene — spawning waves, scoring, scene-wide rules.|
|**GlobalSystem**|The whole app|State that survives scene changes — a game manager, audio settings, save data.|

Pick the smallest scope that fits. Most gameplay is `EntityScript`; reach for `SceneSystem` when you need to work with *many* entities at once, and `GlobalSystem` for things that must persist between scenes.

\---

## Your first script

Here's a complete `EntityScript` that moves its entity with the arrow keys / WASD:

```csharp
using Funke;

public class PlayerController : EntityScript
{
    private float speed = 5.0f;

    public override void OnStart()
    {
        Print("PlayerController attached to: " + Entity.Name);
    }

    public override void OnUpdate()
    {
        var velocity = Vector2.Zero;

        if (Input.GetKey(KeyCode.W)) velocity.Y += 1.0f;
        if (Input.GetKey(KeyCode.S)) velocity.Y -= 1.0f;
        if (Input.GetKey(KeyCode.A)) velocity.X -= 1.0f;
        if (Input.GetKey(KeyCode.D)) velocity.X += 1.0f;

        if (velocity == Vector2.Zero) return;

        Entity.Transform.Position += velocity.Normalized() \* speed \* Time.DeltaTime;
    }
}
```

Notice three things:

* It inherits from `EntityScript`.
* `Entity` refers to the object this script is attached to.
* `Input`, `Time`, and `Transform` are part of the scripting API (covered below).

### Attaching a script to an entity

A script doesn't run until it's attached. In the editor, select an entity, **Add Component → Script** (a `ScriptComponent`), and choose your script class from the list. The engine finds your class, creates an instance, and starts calling its lifecycle methods when the game plays.

\---

## Lifecycle methods

The engine calls these methods at the right moments — you override the ones you need. The exact set varies slightly by script type.

**EntityScript** (per entity):

```csharp
public override void OnAwake() { }          // created, before OnStart
public override void OnStart() { }           // first frame, after everything is loaded
public override void OnUpdate() { }          // every frame
public override void OnFixedUpdate() { }     // every fixed (physics) step
public override void OnDestroy() { }         // entity/script destroyed
public override void OnEnable() { }          // enabled
public override void OnDisable() { }         // disabled

// Physics collisions (need a Rigidbody2D + collider):
public override void OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D c) { }
public override void OnCollisionStay2D(Collision2D c) { }
public override void OnCollisionExit2D(Collision2D c) { }

// Trigger overlaps (one collider has "Sensor" enabled; sensors don't block movement):
public override void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collision2D other) { }
public override void OnTriggerStay2D(Collision2D other) { }
public override void OnTriggerExit2D(Collision2D other) { }
```

Triggers fire on both entities involved, so a pickup can react in its own script (`other.Other` is the entity that touched it). At least one of the two needs a Rigidbody2D, as with collisions.

An entity that starts disabled has no script instances yet. When it is enabled during play (`entity.IsEnabled = true`, or its parent turns on), its scripts are created on the spot: scene field values first, then `OnAwake` and `OnEnable`; `OnStart` follows before its first `OnUpdate`. So code that enables an entity can use `GetComponent<MyScript>()` right away, and values it sets there are not overwritten by the scene.

The same holds for spawning: when `Entity.Instantiate(prefab, position, ...)` returns, the new entity and its children already have their script instances with the prefab's field values, and `OnAwake` and `OnEnable` have run after the clone was placed and parented (so `OnAwake` sees the spawn position). The spawner can configure the copy at once, and `OnStart` still runs before the copy's first `OnUpdate`, so it sees those values:

```csharp
Entity enemy = Entity.Instantiate(EnemyPrefab, spawnPoint, 0f);
enemy.GetComponent<EnemyAI>()!.Target = player;   // not null, also in the same frame
```

`IsEnabled` is the entity's own switch (the Inspector checkbox, Unity's `activeSelf`); `IsEnabledInHierarchy` tells whether it is actually active, i.e. it and all its parents are enabled (`activeInHierarchy`). Enabling a child while its parent is still off keeps the child off until the parent is enabled, so a panel's parts can be switched on before or after the panel itself.

`OnEnable` and `OnDisable` run after the whole hierarchy has switched: inside `OnEnable` both `IsEnabled` and `IsEnabledInHierarchy` read `true`, and in a parent's `OnDisable` its children already read `IsEnabledInHierarchy == false`. A script may switch its own entity off again in `OnEnable` (for example a list row with nothing to show): `Entity.IsEnabled = false` there takes effect, `OnDisable` follows, and children that were switched on in the same step are switched off again before their own `OnEnable` runs.

**SceneSystem** (per scene) has the same `OnAwake` / `OnStart` / `OnUpdate` / `OnFixedUpdate` / `OnDestroy` / `OnEnable` / `OnDisable`, plus a `Scene` property for querying entities — but no collision callbacks.

**GlobalSystem** (whole app) starts with `OnInitialize()` instead of Awake/Start, then `OnUpdate` / `OnFixedUpdate`. It is created at startup and persists across scene loads.

All three also receive application events like `OnApplicationPaused`, `OnApplicationQuit`, and `OnFocusChanged(bool focused)`.

Static fields and static classes keep their values across Play sessions in the editor, because scripts are not reloaded when Play stops. Code that keeps state there (a service, a package) resets it in `Application.PlayModeExited`, which the editor raises after it has restored the scenes.

> The split between `OnUpdate` and `OnFixedUpdate` matters: put \*\*rendering/input-rate\*\* logic in `OnUpdate`, and \*\*physics\*\* logic in `OnFixedUpdate` (which runs at a steady rate regardless of frame rate). See \[Startup Processes](../Engine-Core/Startup-Processes.md#stage-3--the-main-loop).

\---

## Exposing fields to the Inspector

Public fields can be shown and edited in the editor's Inspector using **attributes**. This lets designers tweak values without touching code:

```csharp
public class Enemy : EntityScript
{
    [Header("Stats")]
    [ShowInEditor("Move Speed")] public float Speed = 3.0f;
    [ClampValue(0f, 100f)]       public float Health = 100f;

    [Space(8f)]
    [ToolTip("Seconds between attacks")]
    public float AttackCooldown = 1.5f;

    [EnabledIf("UseCustomColor", true)] public Color Tint = Color.White;
    public bool UseCustomColor = false;

    [HideFromEditor] public int internalCounter;  // public but hidden
}
```

The main attributes:

|Attribute|Effect|
|-|-|
|`[ShowInEditor("Label")]`|Show the field (optionally with a custom label, and read-only if you want).|
|`[ClampValue(min, max)]`|Limit a numeric field to a range.|
|`[Header("Text")]`|A bold section header above the field.|
|`[ToolTip("Text")]`|Hover text explaining the field.|
|`[Space(pixels)]`|Vertical gap for grouping.|
|`[EnabledIf("Field", value)]`|Only enable this field when another field has a given value.|
|`[EditorReadOnly]`|Visible but not editable.|
|`[HideFromEditor]`|Hide a public field.|
|`[EditorIgnore]`|Hide all fields of a whole type.|

You can also expose **asset slots** (a texture, audio clip, etc.) using reference types — drag an asset onto the slot in the Inspector. See [Assets](../Assets/Assets.md#referencing-assets-from-c-scripts).

\---

## What scripts can do (the API)

The `Funke` namespace gives you a broad API. The main categories:

**Entities \& components**

```csharp
Entity e = Entity.FindByName("Boss");
e.GetComponent<SpriteRenderer>();
e.AddComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
Entity copy = Entity.Instantiate(prefab);
e.Destroy();
```

**Transform** — position, rotation, scale:

```csharp
Entity.Transform.Position += Vector2.Up \* Time.DeltaTime;
```

**Hierarchy** — parenting lives on the entity, not the transform:

```csharp
Entity.SetParent(other);          // 'other' is an Entity — keeps world position, rotation and scale (default; 2D and 3D)
Entity.SetParent(other, false);   // adopt the parent's frame instead (local transform kept)
Entity.SetParent(null);           // detach to the scene root
```

**Input** — keyboard, mouse, axes:

The examples below use the core's direct input API. Named actions, control maps and saved keyboard/mouse/gamepad rebinding are available through the optional [Funke.Input](https://github.com/Ben-Scr/Funke-PackageRegistry/tree/main/packages/Funke.Input) package. Install it through the Package Manager and follow its README for the API and version-specific authoring tools. Its namespace is `Funke.InputActions`, distinct from the core `Funke.Input` class.

```csharp
Input.GetKey(KeyCode.Space);        // held
Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space);    // pressed this frame
Input.GetAxis();                    // WASD/arrows, else first gamepad's stick/D-pad, -1..1
Input.MousePosition;

// Mouse look: hide the cursor and read the motion. A locked or hidden cursor stays at the centre of the game view,
// so UI under the crosshair receives hover and clicks.
Cursor.Mode = CursorMode.Locked;   // or Cursor.Visible = false
Vector2 look = Input.GetMouseDelta();
```

In the editor the game holds the mouse only while the focused Game View plays (Play focuses it). **Escape** or switching apps gives the editor its normal OS cursor back and stops mouse-look; `Cursor.Mode` and `Cursor.Visible` keep returning what the game set. Clicking into the Game View (or focusing it) applies the game's cursor mode and custom cursor again, as does a new lock from the game, e.g. when it closes its pause menu.

`Input.MousePosition` and touch positions are pixels of the game's screen, origin top-left. `Application.ScreenWidth` and `ScreenHeight` are that screen's size: in the editor the Game View, not the editor window. To map the pointer onto UI, prefer a RectTransform's resolved `Position` (its centre on that screen) and `Scale` (reference points to pixels) over rebuilding the UI scaling yourself; they include the safe area and the scale mode.

```csharp
// Gamepads (Xbox layout; other pads are mapped automatically). Index 0-3, default 0.
Input.IsGamepadConnected();
Input.GetGamepadButtonDown(GamepadButton.A);
Vector2 move = Input.GetLeftStick();              // deadzone applied (Input.GamepadDeadzone)
float throttle = Input.GetGamepadTrigger(GamepadAxis.RightTrigger); // 0..1

// Touchscreens: every finger (the mouse only follows the first one). Track fingers by Id, not by index.
for (int i = 0; i < Input.TouchCount; i++)
{
    Touch touch = Input.GetTouch(i);   // Id, Position, StartPosition (viewport pixels), Phase
    if (touch.Phase == TouchPhase.Began && IsOverJumpButton(touch.Position)) Jump(); // your own hit test
}

// Phone/tablet motion sensors. Axes follow the rotated screen: +X right, +Y up, +Z out of the screen.
Vector3 tilt = Input.Acceleration;   // in g, toward gravity: (0, -1, 0) upright, (0, 0, -1) lying face up
Vector3 spin = Input.Gyroscope;      // rad/s, counter-clockwise positive
if (Input.IsAccelerometerAvailable) steer = tilt.X; // right edge down → positive
```

UI does not need any of this: Buttons, Toggles and other Interactables respond to every finger, so two on-screen buttons can be held at the same time. Sliders, scrollbars, scroll views, input fields and dropdowns follow the first finger.

The sensors are switched on by the first read (so the first frame can still be zero) and report zero on desktop and Web without such a sensor; Android and iOS need no permission.

**Time**:

```csharp
Time.DeltaTime;        // seconds since last frame (scaled)
Time.FixedDeltaTime;   // physics step length
Time.TimeScale = 0.5f; // slow motion (or 0 to pause game time); clamped to [0, 100]
```

**Window \& frame rate**:

```csharp
Window.IsFullScreen = true;            // also Window.Position, Window.Title, Minimize/Maximize/Restore()
Application.VsyncEnabled = false;      // present without waiting for the monitor refresh
Application.TargetFrameRate = 240;     // frame cap while VSync is off (0 = uncapped)
```

Window changes only apply in the built game: in the editor the game view lives inside the editor's own window, so these calls are ignored there (the Console says so once per call type). With VSync off on Direct3D 12 the game may tear; that is what lets it run above the monitor's refresh rate.

**Physics 2D** — raycasts and overlap checks:

```csharp
RaycastHit2D hit = Physics2D.Raycast(origin, direction, maxDistance);
Entity? hitEntity = Physics2D.OverlapCircle(center, radius);

// Only hit colliders on layers 0 and 3:
RaycastHit2D ground = Physics2D.Raycast(origin, Vector2.Down, 2f, PhysicsLayers.Mask(0, 3));
```

Each collider has a **Layer** (what it is) and **Collides With** (what it touches), both set in the Inspector or from code via `GetComponent<BoxCollider2D>().Layer` / `.CollisionMask`. Two colliders interact only when each one's Collides With includes the other's Layer. `IsSensor`, `Friction`, `Bounciness` and `Density` are available on every collider the same way. `Physics2D.Gravity` sets the world gravity; joints (`RevoluteJoint2D`, `PrismaticJoint2D`, `WeldJoint2D`) and multi-path polygon colliders are described in [Physics 2D](../Engine-Core/Physics-2D.md).

**Sprite animation** — add an **Animator** next to a Sprite Renderer, slice the texture in the Sprite Editor, and record the slices as discrete keys on the clip's **Sprite Renderer > Texture** track. Assign the looping clip to the Animator's **Clip** field. Scripts can change its playback speed:

```csharp
var anim = GetComponent<Animator>();
anim.Speed = 1.5f;
```

Use an Animator Controller to switch between idle, run and other clips; see [Animation](../Engine-Core/Animation.md).

**Tweens** — the **Funke.Tween** package (Package Manager) animates positions, rotations, scales, colors, alpha and any value with a getter and setter over time, DOTween-style: Penner easing, sequences, loops (restart, yoyo, incremental), callbacks, unscaled time for pause menus, shakes and punches. Tweens update themselves and die with their entity:

```csharp
using Funke.Tween;
transform.MoveTo(new Vector2(3f, 0f), 0.5f).SetEase(Ease.OutBack);
panel.ScaleTo(1f, 0.25f).From(0.8f).SetEase(Ease.OutBack).SetUnscaledTime();   // UI pop-in, also while paused
Tween.Sequence().Append(toast.MoveTo(shown, 0.3f)).AppendInterval(2f).Append(background.FadeTo(0f, 0.4f));
Camera2D.Main!.Shake(0.4f, 0.3f);
```

The full API is in the package's [README](https://github.com/Ben-Scr/Funke-PackageRegistry/tree/main/packages/Funke.Tween).

**Multiplayer** — the **Funke.Networking** package (Package Manager) hosts and joins games on every platform: desktop, Android and iOS over UDP, browsers over WebSocket, all on the same server. Messages are structs implementing `INetMessage`; prefabs spawned by the server appear on every client, and a **NetworkTransform2D** / **NetworkTransform3D** script on them keeps their movement smooth on the other screens:

```csharp
using Funke.Networking;
NetworkManager.StartHost();                                    // or StartServer(), StartClient("192.168.0.5")
NetworkServer.ClientConnected += client => NetworkServer.Spawn(PlayerPrefab, Vector3.Zero, 0f, ownerId: client.Id);
NetworkServer.RegisterHandler<Chat>((from, chat) => NetworkServer.SendToAll(chat));
NetworkClient.Send(new Chat { Text = "hi" });
if (NetworkObject.Of(Entity) is { IsOwner: true }) Move();     // scripts run everywhere; only the owner moves
```

Scripts that ship in a package are in **Add Component › Packages**; scenes and prefabs store them by their full class name (`"className": "Funke.Networking.NetworkObject"`). The full API, browser hosting (`wss://`) and internet play are in the package's [README](https://github.com/Ben-Scr/Funke-PackageRegistry/tree/main/packages/Funke.Networking).

**Post-processing** — add **Post Processing** to the entity with the main **Camera 2D** (its *Post Processing* toggle must stay on). `PostProcessing2D` exposes each effect as a value struct: read it, change fields, assign it back.

```csharp
var pp = GetComponent<PostProcessing2D>();

var bloom = pp.Bloom;                 // copy
bloom.Enabled = true;
bloom.Intensity = 3f;
pp.Bloom = bloom;                     // write back — editing pp.Bloom.Intensity directly changes only a copy

var vignette = PostProcessing2D.VignetteSettings.Default;   // Inspector defaults
vignette.Enabled = true;
vignette.Color = new Color(0.4f, 0f, 0f, 1f);
pp.Vignette = vignette;
```

Effects: `ColorGrading`, `Vignette`, `ChromaticAberration`, `Grain`, `Bloom`, `LensDistortion`, `Pixelated`, `GaussianBlur`. Values outside the Inspector ranges are clamped (for example `Bloom.Taps` to 7..500, `ColorGrading.Saturation` to 0..4); the ranges are in each field's XML doc.

**Saving data** — per game, stored under `Application.PersistentDataPath` (`%LOCALAPPDATA%/<Company>/<Name>`, from Project Settings):

```csharp
PlayerPrefs.SetInt("HighScore", 1200);          // small settings and progress values
int best = PlayerPrefs.GetInt("HighScore");     // saved automatically on quit / when Play stops; PlayerPrefs.Save() to force

SaveData.Save("slot1", myInventory);            // any class or struct, written as JSON
if (SaveData.TryLoad("slot1", out Inventory? inv)) { ... }
```

Writes are atomic: a crash while saving leaves the previous file intact.

**Audio**:

```csharp
Audio sfx = Audio.FromAssetUUID(clipGuid);
sfx.PlayOneShot();
Audio.MasterVolume = 0.8f;   // 0..1, everything the game plays (a settings menu's master slider)
```

Automated runs (`--input-script`, `--screenshot-at`, `--quit-at`, `--exec`, `--fixed-step`, `--background` or `--prefs-profile=<name>`, see [Testing](../Editor/Testing.md)) are muted by the engine whatever `MasterVolume` says; `--sound` keeps them audible. `Application.IsAutomatedRun` tells a game it runs under such a test, e.g. to skip an intro or an ad.

**3D sound** — sounds that come from a place in the world. Turn on **Spatial** in an Audio Source's **3D Sound** section: the source then plays from its entity's position (Transform 3D, or Transform 2D at z 0), gets quieter with the distance to the listener and moves to the side it is on. The listener is the main Camera 3D (in 2D games the main Camera 2D), so a sound left of the view is heard on the left. Audio Sources are 2D by default, so existing scenes sound as before.

| Field | Does |
|---|---|
| **Min Distance** | Full volume up to this distance |
| **Max Distance** | **Linear**: silent from here on. **Logarithmic**: the fade stops here |
| **Rolloff** | **Logarithmic** (default): `min / distance` like real sound, half as loud at twice the distance. **Linear**: evenly from full at Min Distance to silent at Max Distance |
| **Panning** | 0 = no left/right, 1 = a sound straight to the side is silent on the other ear (default 0.8) |
| **Doppler Level** | Pitch shift when the source or the listener moves (0 = off, the default) |

```csharp
blast.PlayOneShotAt(center, volume: 1f, minDistance: 8f, maxDistance: 64f);       // a one-shot from a point, Logarithmic
creak.PlayOneShotAt(door, 0.7f, minDistance: 2f, maxDistance: 16f, rolloff: AudioRolloff.Linear);
Audio.PlayOneShotAt(clip, position);                                               // does nothing for a null clip
source.Spatial = true;                                                             // MinDistance, MaxDistance, Rolloff, Panning, DopplerLevel
```

A one-shot that would be silent (Linear, beyond Max Distance) isn't started. To check sounds without listening, for example in muted test runs, the console command `audio.spatial` lists the listener and every playing 3D sound with its distance, gain, pan and left/right volume, and `audio.spatial x y z [min] [max] [log|linear] [panning]` tells how a sound at that point would be heard, without playing it.

**Music and files outside the project** — `AudioStream` plays an MP3, WAV or FLAC file from any path (a music folder, a download) and streams it from disk instead of loading it whole:

```csharp
AudioStream? song = AudioStream.Open(path);      // null if the file is missing or not audio
song?.Play();
song.Time = 42f;                                 // seek; Length, IsPlaying, IsAtEnd, Volume, Loop, Pause(), Stop()
song.Dispose();                                  // e.g. in OnDestroy; leaving Play mode closes every stream
```

**Music / SFX volume settings** — the **Funke.AudioMixer** package (Package Manager) adds volume groups (`Master` → `Music`, `SFX`, plus your own) with mute, fades, snapshots, saved settings and one-line slider binding:

```csharp
using Funke.AudioMixer;
AudioMixer.Attach(musicSource, AudioMixer.Music);        // AudioSource or AudioStream follows the group
AudioMixer.PlayOneShot(clip, AudioMixer.Sfx);
AudioMixer.BindSlider(musicSlider, AudioMixer.Music);    // settings screen; saved to PlayerPrefs
AudioMixer.TransitionTo("Paused", 0.4f);                 // after DefineSnapshot("Paused", (AudioMixer.Music, 0.3f))
```

The full API is in the package's [README](https://github.com/Ben-Scr/Funke-PackageRegistry/tree/main/packages/Funke.AudioMixer).

**Media controls** — for music and podcast apps on Android: a "now playing" notification with cover, previous / play-pause / next and a seek bar, also on the lock screen and for headset buttons. It belongs to the background-audio service, so keep `Audio.PlayInBackground` on while a track is loaded (also when paused) and enable `backgroundAudio` in the Android build profile:

```csharp
MediaSession.SetNowPlaying(track.Title, track.Artist, track.Album, stream.Length, coverRgba, w, h); // on track change
MediaSession.SetPlaybackState(isPlaying, stream.Time);                                            // on play / pause / seek
while (MediaSession.TryGetCommand(out MediaCommand cmd, out float seek)) { /* Play, Pause, Next, Previous, Seek */ }
```

**Images from bytes** — decode a PNG/JPEG you read yourself (a download, cover art embedded in an MP3) into a texture. Decoding is safe on a worker thread; create the texture on the main thread:

```csharp
byte[]? pixels = Texture.DecodeImage(bytes, maxSize: 256, out int w, out int h);
Texture? cover = pixels != null ? Texture.Create(w, h, pixels) : null;   // Dispose() it when done
```

**Background audio** — keep playing when the player switches apps or locks the phone:

```csharp
Audio.PlayInBackground = true;    // while music plays; set false when the user pauses
```

While it's on, sounds keep playing and scripts keep updating (about ten times a second, nothing is rendered) when the game is minimized or in the background. On Android a "playing" notification keeps the app alive; turn on the Android build profile option `backgroundAudio`, and ask for `android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS` on Android 13+ so the notification is visible.

**Permissions on Android** — reading the phone's music or other shared files needs a runtime permission. Ask for it, then poll until the user has answered:

```csharp
Application.RequestPermission("android.permission.READ_MEDIA_AUDIO");
if (Application.GetPermissionState("android.permission.READ_MEDIA_AUDIO") == PermissionState.Granted) { ... }
```

The permission must also be declared in the APK with the Android build profile's `permissions` option ([Platform Packages](../Editor/Platform-Packages.md#android)). Every other platform reports `Granted` right away.

**Logging** — prints to the editor Console:

```csharp
Log.Info("Hello");
Log.Warn("Careful");
Log.Error("Something broke");
```

**Scene queries** — process many entities at once (great in a `SceneSystem`):

```csharp
foreach (ref var t in Scene.QueryRef<NativeTransform2D>())
    t.LocalRotation += Time.DeltaTime;      // spin everything
```

**Math** — `Vector2/3/4`, `Quaternion`, `Color`, `Mathf` (sin, cos, clamp, lerp…), and `Random`. Palette colors go in as written: `Color.FromHex("#3A7BD5")` (also `#RRGGBBAA`, `#RGB`, `#RGBA`; `TryFromHex` doesn't throw, `ToHex()` writes one back).

**UI text:** labels use `Funke.TextRenderer` (`using Funke;`), the same component used for world text. Import `using Funke.UI;` for widgets such as `Button` and `InputField`.

**Text outline and shadow** — a Text Renderer's **Effects** draw an outline and a drop shadow under the glyphs, for world and UI text, sized in text pixels (Font Size units):

```csharp
var label = GetComponent<TextRenderer>()!;
label.OutlineWidth = 3f;                         // 0 = none
label.OutlineColor = Color.FromHex("#1A1026");
label.ShadowOffset = new Vector2(2f, -2f);       // +Y up: down-right; (0, 0) = none
label.ShadowColor = new Color(0f, 0f, 0f, 0.5f);
```

In scene files the keys are `outlineWidth`, `outlineR/G/B/A`, `shadowX`, `shadowY`, `shadowR/G/B/A`. Both effects fade with the text color's alpha.

For heavy parallel work over many entities, combine queries with the [Job System](../Engine-Core/Job-System.md).

